g Connection Diagram) and 'DIAGRAMA DE LIMITES DE DISTÂNCIA DO CABEAMENTO HORIZONTAL' (Horizontal Cabling Distance Limit Diagram) are essential tools that illustrate the complete horizontal cabling channel, showing connections from telecommunication rooms to user devices, including components like patch panels and network outlets [Data: Entities (1632, 1579), Relationships (2618, 2524, 2529)]. These diagrams help in understanding the physical layout and adhering to length limitations to prevent 'PROBLEMAS DE DESEMPENHO DA REDE' (Network Performance Problems) [Data: Entities (1585), Relationships (2544)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Connector Types and Their Application\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Within the network infrastructure, specific connector types are used to ensure reliable connections. 'IDC' (Insulation Displacement Connector) is a common type of connector found in patch panels and network jacks. This connector pierces the wire insulation to establish electrical contact, simplifying the termination process without requiring stripping wires [Data: Entities (1660), Relationships (2696)]. For twisted-pair cabling, 'RJ45' connectors are typically used at the network outlets, providing the interface for connecting patch cords to network devices [Data: Entities (1568, 1600), Relationships (2570)]. The selection and proper application of these connectors are part of the broader 'INFRAESTRUTURA DE CONEXÃO' (Connection Infrastructure) that enables communication within a network [Data: Entities (1583)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Component Lifespan Considerations\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The longevity and durability of network cabling components are also important factors in network design and maintenance. 'VIDA ÚTIL DOS COMPONENTES' (Component Lifespan) refers to the expected durability of items like network jacks, specifying metrics such as the number of insertions and removals they can withstand 